          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            What is known as
            %em Wigner’s theorem
            (in honor of the appendix of §20 in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a{:href => "#Wigner31"} Wigner 1931
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Wigner1959"} 59
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            (generally of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetries
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            (“quantum symmetries”) assuming only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            properties of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"}> pure
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            (namely their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+space"} projectivity
            subject to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Born+rule"} Born rule
            %em deriving
            from this that quantum symmetries are necessarily
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} represented
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operators"} unitary operators
            %em or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-unitary+operators"} anti-unitary operators
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of states
          %p
            Notice that a prominent class of examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-unitary+operators"} anti-unitary operators
            appearing as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+symmetries"} quantum symmetries
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetries
            (
            %a{:href => "#Wigner1959"}> Wigner 1959, §26
            ) such as appearing in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CPT-theorem"} CPT-theorem
          %p
            A key applications of Wigner’s theorem is within the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effectively
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystal
            are identified with possible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+K-theory"} twistings
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            of the crystal’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            (
            %a{:href => "#FreedMoore13"}> Freed &amp; Moore 2013, §1
            , see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Anyonic+topological+order+in+TED+K-theory"} SS22, §2.2
          %h2#Preliminaries Preliminaries
          %p
